const e="2000-old",o=2e3,t="统一卷",a="2000年统一卷",i=[{id:"sec-cloze",type:"cloze",title:"英语知识运用",directions:"Directions: For each numbered blank in the following passage, there are four choices marked ［A］, ［B］, ［C］and ［D］. Choose the best one and mark your answer on ANSWER SHEET 1 by blackening the corresponding letter in the brackets with a pencil. (10 points)",articles:[{id:"cloze-1",title:"完形填空",type:"cloze",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:"If a farmer wishes to succeed, he must try to keep a wide gap between his consumption and his production. He must store a large quantity of grain "},{type:"blank",blankId:1},{type:"paragraph",content:" consuming all his grain immediately. He can continue to support himself and his family "},{type:"blank",blankId:2},{type:"paragraph",content:" he produces a surplus. He must use this surplus in three ways: as seed for sowing, as an insurance "},{type:"blank",blankId:3},{type:"paragraph",content:" the unpredictable effects of bad weather and as a commodity which he must sell in order to "},{type:"blank",blankId:4},{type:"paragraph",content:" old agricultural implements and obtain chemical fertilizers to "},{type:"blank",blankId:5},{type:"paragraph",content:" the soil. He may also need money to construct irrigation "},{type:"blank",blankId:6},{type:"paragraph",content:" and improve his farm in other ways. If no surplus is available, a farmer cannot be "},{type:"blank",blankId:7},{type:"paragraph",content:" . He must either sell some of his property or "},{type:"blank",blankId:8},{type:"paragraph",content:" extra funds in the form of loans. Naturally he will try to borrow money at a low "},{type:"blank",blankId:9},{type:"paragraph",content:" of interest, but loans of this kind are not "},{type:"blank",blankId:10},{type:"paragraph",content:" obtainable."}]}],questions:[{id:1,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"other than",B:"as well as",C:"instead of",D:"more than"},answer:"C",analysis:"instead of 【线索】“储存大量粮食”与“立刻全部吃掉”天然对立 本句是 He must store a large quantity of grain __(1)__ consuming all his grain 与开篇 keep a wide gap between his consumption and his production 也呼应,空格须 【选项】A) other than ✗ 义为“除…之外”,无替代意味 other than通常用于否定句或表“除了”,如 nothing other than…,与本句“储存而非吃掉”的替代关系 B) as well as ✗ 并列“也”,与前后对立冲突 as well as意为“以及、也”,若选此,则等于说“既要储存又要立刻吃掉”,自相矛盾,且违背 keep a wide gap between his consumption and his production 的主旨。 C) instead of ✓ “而不是”,精准对接替代关系 D) more than ✗ “多于”,做比较而非替代 【验证】代入后整句为 He must store a large quantity of grain __(1)__ consuming all his grain 为下文 surplus 的提出做铺垫。",position:0},{id:2,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"only if",B:"much as",C:"long before",D:"ever since"},answer:"A",analysis:"only if 【线索】“继续养家”依赖“有盈余”这一前提 本句 He can continue to support himself and his family __(2)__ he produces a 【选项】 A) only if ✓ “只有当…才”,锁定唯一必要条件 sufficient”形成正反呼应。 B) much as ✗ “尽管”让步,无对立可让 C) long before ✗ “早在…之前”,凭空插入时间点 D) ever since ✗ “自从…以来”,无起点事件 【验证】代回后读作 He can continue to support himself and his family __(2)__ he produces a surplus.,“只有他生产出余粮,他才能继续养家”,与后文 If no surplus is available, a farmer cannot be __(7)__. 一正一反呼应,全段逻辑链通顺。",position:0},{id:3,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"for",B:"against",C:"of",D:"towards"},answer:"B",analysis:"against 【线索】“保险”要对抗的是“恶劣天气的不可预料后果” 本句的余粮第二用途 as an insurance __(3)__ the unpredictable effects of bad 【选项】A) for ✗ “为了”,无防范意味 B) against ✓ 固定搭配 insurance against against与 insurance 构成“防范……”的固定搭配,精准对应“防恶劣天气”这一语义,代入即通。 C) of ✗ “…的保险”,搭配偏弱 D) towards ✗ “朝向”,方向介词,与防范无关 towards表方向或态度,如 attitude towards,与 insurance 不构成自然搭配。 【验证】代入后短语为 as an insurance __(3)__ the unpredictable effects of bad weather 读作",position:0},{id:4,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"replace",B:"purchase",C:"supplement",D:"dispose"},answer:"A",analysis:"replace 【线索】“卖余粮换钱”是为了“处理旧农具并买化肥” 本句 as a commodity which he must sell in order to __(4)__ old agricultural implements and obtain chemical fertilizers to __(5)__ the soil.,卖粮所得有两个用途:对 旧农具做某事、并 obtain(获得)化肥;空 (4) 与 obtain 并列,作用对象是“旧的”农具,常识是 【选项】 A) replace ✓ “替换/更新”,精准匹配“old” replace意为“用新的代替旧的”,与 old agricultural implements 完美对应,且与并列动作 obtain chemical fertilizers(获得新化肥)构成“以新换旧+添新物”的工整结构。 B) purchase ✗ “购买”旧农具不合常识 C) supplement ✗ “补充”,旧农具不需补充 D) dispose ✗ 搭配错:dispose of,且无“换新”含义 dispose表“处理、处置”需接 of(dispose of),本身无“换新”的语义,即便忽略搭配也只表示“扔掉”,跟 【验证】代入后读作 as a commodity which he must sell in order to __(4)__ old agricultural implements and obtain chemical fertilizers to __(5)__ the soil.,“卖余粮以更换旧农具、",position:0},{id:5,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"enhance",B:"mix",C:"feed",D:"raise"},answer:"C",analysis:"feed 【线索】化肥之于土壤=“喂养”土壤 原文 obtain chemical fertilizers to __(5)__ the soil,化肥(fertilizer)对土壤的作用,英语习惯 【选项】A) enhance ✗ “提升”,不接具体物质 soil enhance多接抽象事物(reputation, ability, performance),enhance the soil 不是地道搭配,语义偏 B) mix ✗ “混合”,需 mix sth with the soil mix表混合,要求 mix A with B 结构,直接 mix the soil 语义残缺,且不能表达“施肥养土”的因果。 C) feed ✓ feed the soil 习惯搭配,精准对应化肥施用 feed在农学语境中常与 soil 搭配,意为“给土壤补给养分”,fertilizer feeds the soil 是地道说法,符 D) raise ✗ “提高”或“饲养”,均不接 soil raise可表“提高/饲养/筹集”,但 raise the soil 既不指地力,也不指土壤被抬高,语义不合。 【验证】代入后 obtain chemical fertilizers to __(5)__ the soil 读作“买化肥来滋养土壤”,与上句 replace old agricultural implements(更新农具)并列,共同支撑“余粮换生产投入”的脉络。",position:0},{id:6,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"vessels",B:"routes",C:"paths",D:"channels"},answer:"D",analysis:"channels 【线索】irrigation channels=灌溉水渠,专有搭配 原文 He may also need money to construct irrigation __(6)__ and improve his farm in other ways.,irrigation(灌溉)与 channels 构成固定农业术语“灌溉渠道”,这是地道英语。 【选项】A) vessels ✗ “船只/容器/血管”,语境不符 vessels指轮船、容器或血管,虽然 blood vessels 极常用,但与 irrigation 搭配不通,construct vessels 也不指挖水渠。 B) routes ✗ “线路”,偏抽象路线 C) paths ✗ “小径”,人走的而非水流的 D) channels ✓ irrigation channels 固定表达,精确 channels本义“沟渠/水道”,与 irrigation 构成专业术语“灌溉渠”,construct irrigation channels 是 【验证】代入后 construct irrigation __(6)__ and improve his farm in other ways 读作“修建灌溉",position:0},{id:7,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"self-confident",B:"self-sufficient",C:"self-satisfied",D:"self-restrained"},answer:"B",analysis:"self-sufficient 【线索】“没有余粮就不能自给”是文章核心断言 本句 If no surplus is available, a farmer cannot be __(7)__. 与前句 He can continue to support himself and his family __(2)__ he produces a surplus. 正反对应:有余粮才能养 【选项】A) self-confident ✗ “自信”,与生产盈余无关 B) self-sufficient ✓ “自给自足”,精准对应有/无余粮 self-sufficient意为“自给自足”,直接与前句 He can continue to support himself and his family C) self-satisfied ✗ “自满”,情感色彩负面且偏题 D) self-restrained ✗ “自我克制”,概念跑题 【验证】代入后 If no surplus is available, a farmer cannot be __(7)__. 读作“没有余粮,农民就无法",position:0},{id:8,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"search",B:"save",C:"offer",D:"seek"},answer:"D",analysis:"seek 【线索】“需要钱→寻找额外资金=贷款”,语义自然 本句 He must either sell some of his property or __(8)__ extra funds in the form of 【选项】A) search ✗ “搜查”,需 search for 才接 funds search做及物动词意为“搜查(人/地)”,如 search the house;若表“寻找资金”需 search for,直接 search extra funds 搭配错误。 B) save ✗ “存钱”,与贷款情境冲突 save意为“储存、节省”,但下文 in the form of loans(以贷款形式)明示是借入,不是存,逻辑相反。 C) offer ✗ “提供”,主客颠倒 D) seek ✓ “寻求/谋求”,与 funds/loans 天然搭配 seek意为“寻求”,seek funds/seek loans 是地道搭配,与“以贷款形式获取额外资金”的语义完全 【验证】代回后 He must either sell some of his property or __(8)__ extra funds in the form of",position:0},{id:9,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"proportion",B:"percentage",C:"rate",D:"ratio"},answer:"C",analysis:"rate 【线索】interest rate=利率,经济学固定术语 本句 Naturally he will try to borrow money at a low __(9)__ of interest, but loans of this kind are not __(10)__ obtainable.,a low rate of interest = a low interest rate(低利 【选项】A) proportion ✗ “比例”,不与 interest 搭配 proportion强调“部分与整体之比”,如 a large proportion of,与 interest 不构成术语。 B) percentage ✗ “百分比”,要求具体数字 percentage强调以百分数表达的比率,如 a percentage of 5%,但 a low percentage of interest 不 是行内表达,术语固定为 interest rate。 C) rate ✓ interest rate=利率,固定搭配 rate与 interest 构成经济学固定术语“利率”,a low rate of interest 即“低利率”,精确地道。 D) ratio ✗ “比”,两量相比 ratio指两个量之间的比值,如 a ratio of 2:1,与单一变量“利息率”不匹配。 【验证】代入后 Naturally he will try to borrow money at a low __(9)__ of interest 读作“他自然",position:0},{id:10,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"genuinely",B:"obviously",C:"presumably",D:"frequently"},answer:"D",analysis:"frequently 【线索】but 转折:低息贷款“不常见”才符合现实 本句 Naturally he will try to borrow money at a low __(9)__ of interest, but loans of this kind are not __(10)__ obtainable.,but 暗示“虽然想低息,但现实并非如此”——“低息贷 【选项】A) genuinely ✗ “真正地”,程度副词,语义错位 genuinely强调真实性(如 genuinely happy),not genuinely obtainable 读起来像“并非真正可得”, B) obviously ✗ “显然地”,与 but 转折逻辑冲突 obviously意为显而易见,若说“低息贷款显然不可得”,口吻过于绝对,且与 Naturally he will try 形成 C) presumably ✗ “想必/大概”,推测语气过弱 D) frequently ✓ “经常”,与 not 合成‘并非常能得到’ frequently意为“频繁、经常”,not frequently obtainable=“并不经常能得到”,正好回应农民想要 【验证】代回后 loans of this kind are not __(10)__ obtainable 读作“此类(低息)贷款并不常能取 OVERVIEW · 通读建境 wishes to succeed, he must try to keep a wide gap between his consumption and his 可大致切成三段意群:① 总原则——拉开消费与生产之间的差距(see keep a wide gap between his consumption and his production);② 三大用途列举——as seed for sowing, as an insurance __(3)__ the unpredictable effects of bad weather and as a commodity,并延伸 到construct irrigation __(6)__ and improve his farm in other ways;③ 反面情形——If no surplus is available, a farmer cannot be __(7)__. 只能卖产或借贷,而低息贷款并不易得。把 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 1 Naturally he will try to borrow money at a low rate of interest, but loans of this kind are not frequently obtainable. HIGH-FREQUENCY · 真题高频词 19 support A2 effect A2 v. 支持 n. 影响 本篇1 次 · 真题 87 句 本篇1 次 · 真题 80 句 rate A2 fund B2 n. 速度 n. 资金 本篇1 次 · 真题 72 句 本篇1 次 · 真题 45 句 gap B1 production B1 n. 间隙 n. 生产 本篇1 次 · 真题 45 句 本篇1 次 · 真题 42 句 seek B1 available A2 v. 寻找 adj. 可获得的 本篇1 次 · 真题 40 句 本篇1 次 · 真题 35 句 replace A...",position:0}]},{id:"sec-reading",type:"reading",title:"阅读理解",directions:"Each of the passages below is followed by some questions. For each question there are four answers marked ［A］, ［B］, ［C］and ［D］. Read the passages carefully and choose the best answer to each of the questions. Then mark your answer on ANSWER SHEET 1 by blackening the corresponding letter in the brack",articles:[{id:"text-1",title:"Text 1",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:`A history of long and effortless success can be a dreadful handicap, but, if properly handled, it may become a driving force. When the United States entered just such a glowing period after the end of the Second World War, it had a market eight times larger than any competitor, giving its industries unparalleled economies of scale. Its scientists were the world's best; its workers the most skilled. America and Americans were prosperous beyond the dreams of the Europeans and Asians whose economies the war had destroyed. It was inevitable that this primacy should have narrowed as other countries grew richer. Just as inevitably, the retreat from predominance proved painful. By the mid-1980s Americans had found themselves at a loss over their fading industrial competitiveness. Some huge American industries, such as consumer electronics, had shrunk or vanished in the face of foreign competition. By 1987 there was only one American television maker left, Zenith. (Now there is none: Zenith was bought by South Korea's LG Electronics in July.) Foreign-made cars and textiles were sweeping into the domestic market. America's machine-tool industry was on the ropes. For a while it looked as though the making of semiconductors, which America had invented and which sat at the heart of the new computer age, was going to be the next casualty. All of this caused a crisis of confidence. Americans stopped taking prosperity for granted. They began to believe that their way of doing business was failing, and that their incomes would therefore shortly begin to fall as well. The mid-1980s brought one inquiry after another into the causes of America's industrial decline. Their sometimes sensational findings were filled with warnings about the growing competition from overseas. How things have changed! In 1995 the United States can look back on five years of solid growth while Japan has been struggling. Few Americans attribute this solely to such obvious causes as a devalued dollar or the turning of the business cycle. Self-doubt has yielded to blind pride. "American industry has changed its structure, has gone on a diet, has learnt to be more quick-witted," according to Richard Cavanagh, executive dean of Harvard's Kennedy School of Government. "It makes me proud to be an American just to see how our businesses are improving their productivity," says Stephen Moore of the Cato Institute, a think-tank in Washington, DC. And William Sahlman of the Harvard Business School believes that people will look back on this period as "a golden age of business management in the United States."`}]},{id:"text-2",title:"Text 2",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:'Being a man has always been dangerous. There are about 105 males born for every 100 females, but this ratio drops to near balance at the age of maturity, and among 70-year-olds there are twice as many women as men. But the great universal of male mortality is being changed. Now, boy babies survive almost as well as girls do. This means that, for the first time, there will be an excess of boys in those crucial years when they are searching for a mate. More important, another chance for natural selection has been removed. Fifty years ago, the chance of a baby (particularly a boy baby) surviving depended on its weight. A kilogram too light or too heavy meant almost certain death. Today it makes almost no difference. Since much of the variation is due to genes, one more agent of evolution has gone. There is another way to commit evolutionary suicide: stay alive, but have fewer children. Few people are as fertile as in the past. Except in some religious communities, very few women have 15 children. Nowadays the number of births, like the age of death, has become average. Most of us have roughly the same number of offspring. Again, differences between people and the opportunity for natural selection to take advantage of it have diminished. India shows what is happening. The country offers wealth for a few in the great cities and poverty for the remaining tribal peoples. The grand mediocrity of today—everyone being the same in survival and number of offspring—means that natural selection has lost 80% of its power in upper-middle-class India compared to the tribes. For us, this means that evolution is over; the biological Utopia has arrived. Strangely, it has involved little physical change. No other species fills so many places in nature. But in the past 100,000 years—even the past 100 years—our lives have been transformed but our bodies have not. We did not evolve, because machines and society did it for us. Darwin had a phrase to describe those ignorant of evolution: they "look at an organic being as a savage looks at a ship, as at something wholly beyond his comprehension." No doubt we will remember a 20th century way of life beyond comprehension for its ugliness. But however amazed our descendants may be at how far from Utopia we were, they will look just like us.'}]},{id:"text-3",title:"Text 3",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:'When a new movement in art attains a certain fashion, it is advisable to find out what its advocates are aiming at, for, however farfetched and unreasonable their principles may seem today, it is possible that in years to come they may be regarded as normal. With regard to Futurist poetry, however, the case is rather difficult, for whatever Futurist poetry may be—even admitting that the theory on which it is based may be right—it can hardly be classed as Literature. This, in brief, is what the Futurist says: for a century, past conditions of life have been conditionally speeding up, till now we live in a world of noise and violence and speed. Consequently, our feelings, thoughts and emotions have undergone a corresponding change. This speeding up of life, says the Futurist, requires a new form of expression. We must speed up our literature too, if we want to interpret modern stress. We must pour out a large stream of essential words, unhampered by stops, or qualifying adjectives, or finite verbs. Instead of describing sounds we must make up words that imitate them; we must use many sizes of type and different colored inks on the same page, and shorten or lengthen words at will. Certainly their descriptions of battles are confused. But it is a little upsetting to read in the explanatory notes that a certain line describes a fight between a Turkish and a Bulgarian officer on a bridge off which they both fall into the river—and then to find that the line consists of the noise of their falling and the weights of the officers: "Pluff! Pluff! A hundred and eighty-five kilograms." This, though it fulfills the laws and requirements of Futurist poetry, can hardly be classed as Literature. All the same, no thinking man can refuse to accept their first proposition: that a great change in our emotional life calls for a change of expression. The whole question is really this: have we essentially changed?'}]},{id:"text-4",title:"Text 4",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:`Aimlessness has hardly been typical of the postwar Japan whose productivity and social harmony are the envy of the United States and Europe. But increasingly the Japanese are seeing a decline of the traditional work- moral values. Ten years ago young people were hardworking and saw their jobs as their primary reason for being, but now Japan has largely fulfilled its economic needs, and young people don't know where they should go next. The coming of age of the postwar baby boom and an entry of women into the male-dominated job market have limited the opportunities of teenagers who are already questioning the heavy personal sacrifices involved in climbing Japan's rigid social ladder to good schools and jobs. In a recent survey, it was found that only 24.5 percent of Japanese students were fully satisfied with school life, compared with 67.2 percent of students in the United States. In addition, far more Japanese workers expressed dissatisfaction with their jobs than did their counterparts in the 10 other countries surveyed. While often praised by foreigners for its emphasis on the basics, Japanese education tends to stress test taking and mechanical learning over creativity and self-expression. "Those things that do not show up in the test scores—personality, ability, courage or humanity—are completely ignored," says Toshiki Kaifu, chairman of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's education committee. "Frustration against this kind of thing leads kids to drop out and run wild." Last year Japan experienced 2,125 incidents of school violence, including 929 assaults on teachers. Amid the outcry, many conservative leaders are seeking a return to the prewar emphasis on moral education. Last year Mitsuo Setoyama, who was then education minister, raised eyebrows when he argued that liberal reforms introduced by the American occupation authorities after World War II had weakened the "Japanese morality of respect for parents." But that may have more to do with Japanese life-styles. "In Japan," says educator Yoko Muro, "it's never a question of whether you enjoy your job and your life, but only how much you can endure." With economic growth has come centralization; fully 76 percent of Japan's 119 million citizens live in cities where community and the extended family have been abandoned in favor of isolated, two-generation households. Urban Japanese have long endured lengthy commutes (travels to and from work) and crowded living conditions, but as the old group and family values weaken, the discomfort is beginning to tell. In the past decade, the Japanese divorce rate, while still well below that of the United States, has increased by more than 50 percent, and suicides have increased by nearly one-quarter.`}]},{id:"text-5",title:"Text 5",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:`If ambition is to be well regarded, the rewards of ambition—wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny— must be deemed worthy of the sacrifices made on ambition's behalf. If the tradition of ambition is to have vitality, it must be widely shared; and it especially must be highly regarded by people who are themselves admired, the educated not least among them. In an odd way, however, it is the educated who have claimed to have given up on ambition as an ideal. What is odd is that they have perhaps most benefited from ambition—if not always their own then that of their parents and grandparents. There is a heavy note of hypocrisy in this, a case of closing the barn door after the horses have escaped—with the educated themselves riding on them. Certainly people do not seem less interested in success and its signs now than formerly. Summer homes, European travel, BMWs—the locations, place names and name brands may change, but such items do not seem less in demand today than a decade or two years ago. What has happened is that people cannot confess fully to their dreams, as easily and openly as once they could, lest they be thought pushing, acquisitive and vulgar. Instead, we are treated to fine hypocritical spectacles, which now more than ever seem in ample supply: the critic of American materialism with a Southampton summer home; the publisher of radical books who takes his meals in three-star restaurants; the journalist advocating participatory democracy in all phases of life, whose own children are enrolled in private schools. For such people and many more perhaps not so exceptional, the proper formulation is, "Succeed at all costs but avoid appearing ambitious." The attacks on ambition are many and come from various angles; its public defenders are few and unimpressive, where they are not extremely unattractive. As a result, the support for ambition as a healthy impulse, a quality to be admired and fixed in the mind of the young, is probably lower than it has ever been in the United States. This does not mean that ambition is at an end, that people no longer feel its stirrings and promptings, but only that, no longer openly honored, it is less openly professed. Consequences follow from this, of course, some of which are that ambition is driven underground, or made sly. Such, then, is the way things stand: on the left angry critics, on the right stupid supporters, and in the middle, as usual, the majority of earnest people trying to get on in life.`}]}],questions:[{id:11,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"The U.S. achieved its predominance after World War II because____.",options:{A:"it had made painstaking efforts towards this goal",B:"its domestic market was eight times larger than before",C:"the war had destroyed the economies of most potential competitors",D:"the unparalleled size of its workforce had given an impetus to its economy"},answer:"C",analysis:"【判型】题干以 because 句末待补全的结构出现，要求从四个选项里选出符合文意的原因。本质是 【拆句】题干 The U.S. achieved its predominance after World War II because\\_\\_\\_\\_ 是不完整 陈述句，主干 The U.S. achieved its predominance；after World War II 是时间状语； 【定位】P1 America and Americans were prosperous beyond the dreams of the Europeans and Asians whose economies the war had destroyed. 【选项】A) 无中生有 long and effortless success。 原文 a market eight times larger than any competitor，比较对象是”任何对手”；选 项偷改为 eight times larger than before（比”以前”大），且把”市场规模”当成原因本 C) ✓ 正确 the Europeans and Asians whose economies the war had destroyed ↔ the war had destroyed the economies of most potential competitors the Europeans and Asians ↔ most potential competitors C 项把定位句 whose economies the war had destroyed 的因果完整重写，主语、宾 原文中 unparalleled 修饰的是 economies of scale（规模经济），而非 workforce 的 规模；且原文称工人是 the most skilled（最熟练），未提其 size。",position:0},{id:12,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"The loss of U.S. predominance in the world economy in the 1980s is manifested in the fact that the American____.",options:{A:"TV industry had withdrawn to its domestic market",B:"semiconductor industry had been taken over by foreign enterprises",C:"machine-tool industry had collapsed after suicidal actions",D:"auto industry had lost part of its domestic market"},answer:"D",analysis:"【判型】题干 is manifested in the fact that the American\\_\\_\\_\\_ 要求选出原文中体现衰退的具 【拆句】题干 The loss of U.S. predominance in the world economy in the 1980s is manifested in the fact that the American\\_\\_\\_\\_ 主干为 The loss is manifested in the fact；that 引导同位语从句补足 fact 内容，American 后接的名词由选项补全。注意 manifested in 意 【定位】P2 Foreign-made cars and textiles were sweeping into the domestic market. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 原文 By 1987 there was only one American television maker left, Zenith. (Now there is none: Zenith was bought by South Korea's LG Electronics in July.) 说的是 原文 For a while it looked as though the making of semiconductors...was going to be the next casualty. 是”一度看起来”会成为下一个牺牲品，是担忧/可能性，且文章 原文 America's machine-tool industry was on the ropes. 意为”陷入困境/濒临崩 D) ✓ 正确 Foreign-made cars and textiles were sweeping into the domestic market. ↔ auto industry had lost part of its domestic market cars ↔ auto industry D 项是原文”外车涌入本土市场”事实的视角反转表达，且只说 lost part of its domestic market（部分份额），与原文 sweeping into 的语义强度相符。",position:0},{id:13,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"What can be inferred from the passage?",options:{A:"It is human nature to shift between self-doubt and blind pride.",B:"Intense competition may contribute to economic progress.",C:"The revival of the economy depends on international cooperation.",D:"A long history of success may pave the way for further development."},answer:"B",analysis:"【判型】题干 What can be inferred from the passage? 字面给出 inferred，指向全篇推断。 【拆句】题干 What can be inferred from the passage? 是简单一般疑问句，被动语态 can be 【选项】A) 过度推断 原文确有 crisis of confidence 与 Self-doubt has yielded to blind pride. 的情绪变化 B) ✓ 正确 Americans had found themselves at a loss over their fading industrial competitiveness. ↔ Intense competition may contribute to economic progress. All of this caused a crisis of confidence. ↔ may contribute to economic progress American industry has changed its structure, has gone on a diet, has learnt to be more quick- witted ↔ contribute to economic progress 全文谈的是 international competition（外部竞争），并未提及任何 international 全文开篇即点出 A history of long and effortless success can be a dreadful",position:0},{id:14,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"The author seems to believe the revival of the U.S. economy in the 1990s can be attributed to the____.",options:{A:"turning of the business cycle",B:"restructuring of industry",C:"improved business management",D:"success in education"},answer:"A",analysis:"【判型】题干 can be attributed to the\\_\\_\\_\\_ 要求选出作者归结的原因，本质是细节核对作者旁 【拆句】题干 The author seems to believe the revival of the U.S. economy in the 1990s can be attributed to the\\_\\_\\_\\_ 主干 The author believes（that 省略）the revival can be attributed to ...；attributed to 意为”归因于”，注意 seems to 暗示作者并非斩钉截铁，但 【定位】P4 Few Americans attribute this solely to such obvious causes as a devalued dollar or the turning of the business cycle. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 the turning of the business cycle ↔ turning of the business cycle Few Americans attribute this solely to such obvious causes ↔ turning of the business cycle A 项是定位句里原词照搬的归因选项，作者通过 Few...solely 与 obvious causes 暗示自 American industry has changed its structure 出自 Richard Cavanagh之口，属直接 our businesses are improving their productivity 出自 Stephen Moore；a golden age of business management 出自 William Sahlman。这些”管理改善”评价都来自被 全文未出现 education 或与教育相关的任何陈述；第 1 段提到 scientists were the world's best; its workers the most skilled，但说的是战后状态，不是 1990 年代复苏 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 13 A history of long and effortless success can be a dreadful handicap, but, if properly handled, it may become a driving force. When the United States entered just such a glowing period after the end of the Second World War, it had a market eight times larger than any competitor, giving its industries unparalleled economies of scale. America and Americans were prosperous beyond the dreams of the Europeans and Asians whose economies the war had destroyed. By the mid-1980s Americans had found themselves...",position:0},{id:15,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"What used to be the danger in being a man according to the first paragraph?",options:{A:"A lack of mates.",B:"A fierce competition.",C:"A lower survival rate.",D:"A defective gene."},answer:"C",analysis:'【判型】What used to be the danger in being a man 问的是"过去那种危险具体是什么"，是回原 【拆句】What used to be the danger in being a man according to the first paragraph 是一个 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主干是 What used to be the danger，used to be 表示"过去 曾经是"，暗含"现在已不再如此"的对比；according to the first paragraph 是状语，限定 【定位】P1 Being a man has always been dangerous. There are about 105 males born for every 100 females, but this ratio drops to near balance at the age of maturity, and among 70-year-olds there are twice as many women as men. 【选项】A) 时间错位类干扰 A lack of mates 利用了 there will be an excess of boys in those crucial years when they are searching for a mate，但原文说的是 "将来" 会男多女少（for the first time），是变化后的新情况，不是 What used to be the danger 问的过去的危险。 A fierce competition 在 P1 找不到对应；原文只谈男婴出生率、男性死亡率、出生体 C) ✓ 正确 among 70-year-olds there are twice as many women as men ↔ A lower survival rate Now, boy babies survive almost as well as girls do ↔ A lower survival rate 预判出"男性活下来概率更低"，正好就是 A lower survival rate 的中文翻译；C 是定位 A defective gene 利用了 Since much of the variation is due to genes, one more agent of evolution has gone，但原文说的是"出生体重的差异由基因决定"，根本没说 男性有缺陷基因——variation due to genes ≠ defective gene。',position:0},{id:16,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"What does the example of India illustrate?",options:{A:"Wealthy people tend to have fewer children than poor people.",B:"Natural selection hardly works among the rich and the poor.",C:"The middle class population is 80% smaller than that of the tribes.",D:"India is one of the countries with a very high birth rate."},answer:"B",analysis:'【判型】What does the example of India illustrate 明确把 India 定性为"例子"，问的是它服务于 【拆句】What does the example of India illustrate 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主干 What does the example illustrate，of India 是后置定语限定 example。无陷阱。 【定位】P2 The grand mediocrity of today—everyone being the same in survival and number of offspring —means that natural selection has lost 80% of its power in upper-middle-class India compared to the tribes. 【选项】A) 答非所问 Wealthy people tend to have fewer children than poor people 哪怕属实，也是例 B) ✓ 正确 natural selection has lost 80% of its power in upper-middle-class India compared to the tribes ↔ Natural selection hardly works among the rich and the poor Again, differences between people and the opportunity for natural selection to take advantage of it have diminished ↔ Natural selection hardly works among the rich and the poor 预判出"贫富两端自然选择都失效"，正好是 Natural selection hardly works among the rich and the poor 的字面意思——B 是 India 例子真正要证明的论点。 The middle class population is 80% smaller than that of the tribes 利用了 natural selection has lost 80% of its power in upper-middle-class India compared to the tribes 里的 80%，但原文 80% 修饰的是 power（自然选择的力量），不是 population（人口）。 India is one of the countries with a very high birth rate 在 P2 完全无对应；原文谈',position:0},{id:17,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"The author argues that our bodies have stopped evolving because____.",options:{A:"life has been improved by technological advance",B:"the number of female babies has been declining",C:"our species has reached the highest stage of evolution",D:"the difference between wealth and poverty is disappearing"},answer:"A",analysis:'【判型】The author argues that our bodies have stopped evolving because 形式上是补全句 【拆句】The author argues that our bodies have stopped evolving because____ 是一个待补全 陈述句。主句 The author argues + that 宾语从句 our bodies have stopped evolving， stop doing 表"停止做某事"；because 引导原因状语从句，从句缺失，由四个选项补出。 【定位】P3 But in the past 100,000 years—even the past 100 years—our lives have been transformed but our bodies have not. We did not evolve, because machines and society did it for us. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 machines and society did it for us ↔ life has been improved by technological advance our lives have been transformed but our bodies have not ↔ life has been improved by technological advance 预判出"机器与社会替进化做了事"，正好就是 life has been improved by technological advance 的实质——technological advance 是机器和社会的总称， improved life 是它带来的结果。 the number of female babies has been declining 与 P1 中 105 男婴比 100 女婴的 declining；与 Q17 因果完全无关。 our species has reached the highest stage of evolution 利用了 the biological Utopia has arrived，但 Utopia 在原文是带反讽口吻的措辞（紧接着作者就吐槽 our descendants may be at how far from Utopia we were）——原文从未说人类已是进 the difference between wealth and poverty is disappearing 利用了 The grand mediocrity of today 的语义，但作者说的是"存活与子女数趋同"，不是"贫富差异在消',position:0},{id:18,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"Which of the following would be the best title for the passage?",options:{A:"Sex Ratio Changes in Human Evolution.",B:"Ways of Continuing Man's Evolution.",C:"The Evolutionary Future of Nature.",D:"Human Evolution Going Nowhere."},answer:"D",analysis:`【判型】Which of the following would be the best title for the passage 问的是全文最佳标题， 【拆句】Which of the following would be the best title for the passage 是一个 Which 引导的 特殊疑问句，would 是虚拟语气表"在所给选项中应是"，主干 Which would be the best title。无陷阱。 【选项】A) 以偏概全 Sex Ratio Changes in Human Evolution 只触到 There are about 105 males born for every 100 females 这部分内容，P2 与 P3 完全装不进，作为全文标题严重以偏 Ways of Continuing Man's Evolution 默认了进化可以"继续"并给出方法，但全文论断 恰恰是 evolution is over——作者根本不主张"如何继续"。 The Evolutionary Future of Nature 利用了 No other species fills so many places in nature 里的 nature 一词，但全文真正讨论的对象是 human/man，不是 nature 整 D) ✓ 正确 evolution is over ↔ Human Evolution Going Nowhere natural selection has lost 80% of its power ↔ Human Evolution Going Nowhere another chance for natural selection has been removed ↔ Human Evolution Going Nowhere D 项的 Human Evolution 覆盖全文话题，Going Nowhere 同时对接 P1、P2、P3 三 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 11 There are about 105 males born for every 100 females, but this ratio drops to near balance at the age of maturity, and among 70-year-olds there are twice as many women as men. Now, boy babies survive almost as well as girls do. Today it makes almost no difference. Since much of the variation is due to genes, one more agent of evolution has gone. 第1段，第10句 There is another way to commit evolutionary suicide: stay alive, but have fewer children. Few people are as fertile as in the past. Again, differences between people and the opportunity for natural selection to take advantage of it have diminish...`,position:0},{id:19,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"This passage is mainly____.",options:{A:"a survey of new approaches to art",B:"a review of Futurist poetry",C:"about merits of the Futurist movement",D:"about laws and requirements of literature"},answer:"B",analysis:'【判型】This passage is mainly 同时具备主旨标记 mainly 与范围词 passage，是教科书式的主旨 【拆句】This passage is mainly____. 是主系表残缺结构，空格紧跟系动词 is 后的状语 mainly，四 【选项】A) 以偏概全（反向：把局部对象扩大） 全文只评 Futurist poetry 这一种新艺术运动，并非泛泛 a survey of new approaches B) ✓ 正确 With regard to Futurist poetry ↔ a review of Futurist poetry can hardly be classed as Literature ↔ a review of Futurist poetry a review of Futurist poetry 既圈定了正确对象，也以 review 涵盖了全文"陈述主张 + 全文对 Futurist 诗歌持否定态度（it can hardly be classed as Literature、can hardly be classed as Literature），不可能是 about merits of the Futurist movement"谈未 原文 fulfills the laws and requirements of Futurist poetry 谈的是 Futurist 诗歌内部 规则，不是 laws and requirements of literature"文学一般规则"；全文也未系统阐述',position:0},{id:20,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"When a novel literary idea appears, people should try to____.",options:{A:"determine its purposes",B:"ignore its flaws",C:"follow the new fashions",D:"accept the principles"},answer:"A",analysis:'【判型】people should try to 要求填一个具体动作，是对原文某句客观陈述的复述，不需要推断， 【拆句】When a novel literary idea appears, people should try to____. 是 when 时间状语从 【定位】P1 When a new movement in art attains a certain fashion, it is advisable to find out what its advocates are aiming at, for, however farfetched and unreasonable their principles may seem today, it is possible that in years to come they may be regarded as normal. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 find out what its advocates are aiming at ↔ determine its purposes 预判答案是"弄清倡导者的目的"，determine its purposes 直接对应这一语义，是定位 原文要求 find out what its advocates are aiming at 是认知层面的弄清，而 follow the new fashions 是行为层面的追随，二者语义不同。 原文只让人 find out（弄清）主张，并未要求 accept the principles（接受主张），且',position:0},{id:21,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"Futurists claim that we must____.",options:{A:"increase the production of literature",B:"use poetry to relieve modern stress",C:"develop new modes of expression",D:"avoid using adjectives and verbs"},answer:"C",analysis:'【判型】Futurists claim that we must____. 问的是 Futurists 明确提出的具体主张，属对原文陈述 【拆句】Futurists claim that we must____. 是主句 + that 宾语从句，从句主语 we 后接情态动词 【定位】P2 We must speed up our literature too, if we want to interpret modern stress. We must pour out a large stream of essential words, unhampered by stops, or qualifying adjectives, or finite verbs. Instead of describing sounds we must make up words that imitate them; we must use many sizes of type and different colored inks on the same page, and shorten or lengthen words at will. 【选项】A) 偷换概念 原文 We must speed up our literature too 是"加快文学（节奏）"，并非 increase the production of literature"增加文学产量"，二者完全不同。 原文是 if we want to interpret modern stress——"为了表达现代张力"才要改造文 学，而 use poetry to relieve modern stress 却说"用诗歌来缓解现代张力"，把"表 C) ✓ 正确 We must speed up our literature too, if we want to interpret modern stress ↔ develop new modes of expression This speeding up of life, says the Futurist, requires a new form of expression ↔ develop new modes of expression 预判答案是"为文学发明新的表达形式"，develop new modes of expression 直接对应 requires a new form of expression，且能覆盖第二段所有 we must 句的公约数。 原文 unhampered by stops, or qualifying adjectives, or finite verbs 说的是新文学 不被句号、修饰形容词、限定动词"拖累"，并非全面 avoid using adjectives and verbs ——既漏掉了 stops，又把"qualifying adjectives / finite verbs"扩大成所有 adjectives and verbs。',position:0},{id:22,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"The author believes that Futurist poetry is____.",options:{A:"based on reasonable principles",B:"new and acceptable to ordinary people",C:"indicative of a basic change in human nature",D:"more of a transient phenomenon than literature"},answer:"D",analysis:'【判型】The author believes that Futurist poetry is____ 直接问"作者认为 X 是怎样的"，是标准的 【拆句】The author believes that Futurist poetry is____. 是主句 + that 宾语从句，从句"Futurist 【选项】A) 与原文相反 原文 however farfetched and unreasonable their principles may seem today 反而 把其原则贴上"牵强不合理"的标签，与 based on reasonable principles 直接相反。 原文从未讨论 Futurist 诗歌是否 new and acceptable to ordinary people"为普通人所 原文 have we essentially changed? 明白把"人是否本质改变"作为未决之问；C 项却让 作者断言 Futurist 诗歌 indicative of a basic change in human nature，把疑问句直 D) ✓ 正确 it can hardly be classed as Literature ↔ more of a transient phenomenon than literature have we essentially changed? ↔ more of a transient phenomenon than literature more of a transient phenomenon than literature 同时命中"不算真正的文学"与"短 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 7 When a new movement in art attains a certain fashion, it is advisable to find out what its advocates are aiming at, for, however farfetched and unreasonable their principles may seem today, it is possible that in years to come they may be regarded as normal. With regard to Futurist poetry, however, the case is rather difficult, for whatever Futurist poetry may be—even admitting that the theory on which it is based may be right—it can hardly be classed as Literature. This, in brief, is what the Futurist says: for a century, past conditions of life have been conditionally speeding up, till now we live in a world of noise and violence and speed. 间状语从句,都是考试常见句式。特别是"This is what..."和"have been doing till now"两个模式实用 Consequently, our feelings,...',position:0},{id:23,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"In the Westerners' eyes, the postwar Japan was____.",options:{A:"under aimless development",B:"a positive example",C:"a rival to the West",D:"on the decline"},answer:"B",analysis:`【判型】题干 In the Westerners' eyes, the postwar Japan was____ 是对客观事实（西方人如何看 待战后日本）的判断，未出现 infer/suggest/imply 等推理动词。 【拆句】句子主干为 the postwar Japan was____，前置状语 In the Westerners' eyes 表视角；考 生需理解 the postwar Japan 是被评价对象、Westerners 是评价主体，二者一旦对调即整 【定位】P1 Aimlessness has hardly been typical of the postwar Japan whose productivity and social harmony are the envy of the United States and Europe. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 under aimless development 即"漫无目的地发展"，而原文明确说 Aimlessness has hardly been typical of the postwar Japan，A 项与原文极性相反。 B) ✓ 正确 the envy of the United States and Europe ↔ a positive example Aimlessness has hardly been typical of the postwar Japan ↔ a positive example on the decline 指"在走下坡路"，而原文说战后日本生产率与社会和谐 the envy of the United States and Europe，是上升/榜样状态；本段后文谈"工作道德下滑"是另一话`,position:0},{id:24,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"According to the author, what may chiefly be responsible for the moral decline of Japanese society?",options:{A:"Women's participation in social activities is limited.",B:"More workers are dissatisfied with their jobs.",C:"Excessive emphasis has been placed on the basics.",D:"The life-style has been influenced by Western values."},answer:"D",analysis:`【判型】According to the author, what may chiefly be responsible for 要求按作者明示的归因作 【拆句】情态动词 may 与 chiefly 共同限定 be responsible for：may 留出措辞余地、chiefly 排除 【定位】P4 But that may have more to do with Japanese life-styles. "In Japan," says educator Yoko Muro, "it's never a question of whether you enjoy your job and your life, but only how much you can endure." With economic growth has come centralization; fully 76 percent of Japan's 119 million citizens live in cities where community and the extended family have been abandoned in favor of isolated, two-generation households. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 Women's participation in social activities is limited. 与原文 an entry of women into the male-dominated job market 极性相反——原文说女性正在进入职场，而非 More workers are dissatisfied with their jobs. 对应 far more Japanese workers expressed dissatisfaction with their jobs，原文有此事实，但它只是道德下滑的表现/ 伴随现象，不是作者明示的"主因"——主因明示在第 4 段 that may have more to do with Japanese life-styles。 Excessive emphasis has been placed on the basics. 把 often praised by foreigners for its emphasis on the basics 的"外国人对基础教育的赞赏"偷换成"过度强调基础"； 原文用 often praised 是肯定语气，并未说 excessive，且这是教育层面而非道德下滑 D) ✓ 正确 With economic growth has come centralization; fully 76 percent of Japan's 119 million citizens live in cities where community and the extended family have been abandoned in favor of isolated, two-generation households ↔ The life-style has been influenced by Western values. But that may have more to do with Japanese life-styles ↔ The life-style has been influenced by ...`,position:0},{id:25,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"Which of the following is true according to the author____?",options:{A:"Japanese education is praised for helping the young climb the social ladder.",B:"Japanese education is characterized by mechanical learning as well as creativity.",C:"More stress should be placed on the cultivation of creativity.",D:"Dropping out leads to frustration against test taking."},answer:"C",analysis:`【判型】Which of the following is true according to the author 四个选项均是关于日本教育的事 【拆句】题干为不完整疑问 Which of the following is true according to the author____，下划线 author 是限定状语，过滤掉 Kaifu 等他人引语。 【定位】P3 While often praised by foreigners for its emphasis on the basics, Japanese education tends to stress test taking and mechanical learning over creativity and self-expression. 【选项】A) 无中生有 Japanese education is praised for helping the young climb the social ladder. 原文 often praised by foreigners for its emphasis on the basics 中赞赏的内容是"重视基 础"，并非"帮助年轻人攀登社会阶梯"；社会阶梯一词出现在 climbing Japan's rigid Japanese education is characterized by mechanical learning as well as creativity. 把 mechanical learning 与 creativity 并列；原文明确二者对立——stress test taking and mechanical learning over creativity and self-expression 与 personality, ability, courage or humanity—are completely ignored。 C) ✓ 正确 Japanese education tends to stress test taking and mechanical learning over creativity and self- expression ↔ More stress should be placed on the cultivation of creativity. "Those things that do not show up in the test scores—personality, ability, courage or humanity— are completely ignored," ↔ More stress should be placed on the cultivation of creativity. 预判"作者主张抬高创造力培养"与 More stress should be placed on the cultivation Dropping out leads to frustration against test taking. 把原文 Frustration against this kind of thing leads kids to drop out and run wild. 的因果颠倒——原文是"对应`,position:0},{id:26,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"The change in Japanese life-style is revealed in the fact that____.",options:{A:"the young are less tolerant of discomforts in life",B:"the divorce rate in Japan exceeds that in the U.S.",C:"the Japanese endure more than ever before",D:"the Japanese appreciate their present life"},answer:"A",analysis:'【判型】The change in Japanese life-style is revealed in the fact that____ 要求选出原文已陈述 【拆句】句子主干 The change...is revealed in the fact that____，that 引导同位语从句承载"事实 【定位】P4 In the past decade, the Japanese divorce rate, while still well below that of the United States, has increased by more than 50 percent, and suicides have increased by nearly one-quarter. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 as the old group and family values weaken, the discomfort is beginning to tell ↔ the young are less tolerant of discomforts in life the Japanese divorce rate, while still well below that of the United States, has increased by more than 50 percent, and suicides have increased by nearly one-quarter ↔ the young are less tolerant of discomforts in life 预判"对不适忍耐力下降"与 the young are less tolerant of discomforts in life 完全同 the divorce rate in Japan exceeds that in the U.S.（日本离婚率超过美国）与原文 still well below that of the United States 直接相反——原文明示日本离婚率仍"远低 the Japanese endure more than ever before（日本人比以前更能忍）与原文 the discomfort is beginning to tell 与 Muro 引语 only how much you can endure 的语 the Japanese appreciate their present life（欣赏当下生活）与全段基调相反——原文 用 lengthy commutes、crowded living conditions、divorce rate...increased by more than 50 percent、suicides have increased 渲染负面状态。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 6 Aimlessness has hardly been typical of the postwar Japan whose productivity and social harmony are the envy of the United States and Europe. Ten years ago young people were hardworking and saw their jobs as their primary reason for being, but ...',position:0},{id:27,articleId:"text-5",type:"reading",question:"It is generally believed that ambition may be well regarded if____.",options:{A:"its returns well compensate for the sacrifices",B:"it is rewarded with money, fame and power",C:"its goals are spiritual rather than material",D:"it is shared by the rich and the famous"},answer:"A",analysis:`【判型】It is generally believed that ambition may be well regarded if____. 是补全句，要求从 原文找出 "ambition 被认可" 所附带的条件，无 infer/imply 等推断信号。 【拆句】It is generally believed that ambition may be well regarded if____. 是 "It is believed that+主句+if 条件状语从句" 结构，重心在 if 之后——补全的是 ambition 被认可所需的条 【定位】P1 If ambition is to be well regarded, the rewards of ambition—wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny—must be deemed worthy of the sacrifices made on ambition's behalf. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 the rewards of ambition—wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny—must be deemed worthy of the sacrifices made on ambition's behalf ↔ its returns well compensate for the sacrifices its returns well compensate for the sacrifices 把定位句拆出的 "回报抵得上牺牲" 改 it is rewarded with money, fame and power 只复述了原文 wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny 的列举，丢掉了原文最关键的条件 "deemed worthy of the sacrifices"——条件不见了，命题就不成立。 原文列出的回报 wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny 全是物质或世俗目 标；its goals are spiritual rather than material 把回报说成 "精神而非物质"，正好与 it is shared by the rich and the famous 谈的是"被谁分享"，与题干所问 "何时被认可" 的条件无关；原文谈的是 "广泛分享 + 受 educated 推崇"（it must be widely shared; and it especially must be highly regarded by people who are themselves admired, the educated not least among them.），并非 "the rich and the famous"。`,position:0},{id:28,articleId:"text-5",type:"reading",question:"The last sentence of the first paragraph most probably implies that it is____.",options:{A:"customary of the educated to discard ambition in words",B:"too late to check ambition once it has been let out",C:"dishonest to deny ambition after the fulfillment of the goal",D:"impractical for the educated to enjoy benefits from ambition"},answer:"C",analysis:'【判型】most probably implies 是典型推断题信号词，问的是字面之外的言外之意。 【拆句】The last sentence of the first paragraph most probably implies that it is____. 是 "主语 【定位】P1 There is a heavy note of hypocrisy in this, a case of closing the barn door after the horses have escaped—with the educated themselves riding on them. 【选项】A) 以偏概全 customary of the educated to discard ambition in words 只说"惯于口头放弃"，把 原文里最关键的 heavy note of hypocrisy 的伪善评价丢了——隐喻句的精髓是"事 too late to check ambition once it has been let out 只翻译了 closing the barn door after the horses have escaped 的字面（为时已晚），把作者真正要类比的本体 check（约束）ambition。 C) ✓ 正确 a heavy note of hypocrisy in this, a case of closing the barn door after the horses have escaped— with the educated themselves riding on them ↔ dishonest to deny ambition after the fulfillment of the goal 原句要点有二——hypocrisy（伪善/不诚实）+ already benefited（已经享受）； dishonest to deny ambition after the fulfillment of the goal 两点全中。 原文恰恰说 they have perhaps most benefited from ambition——受教育者最受益 于 ambition；impractical for the educated to enjoy benefits from ambition 反过',position:0},{id:29,articleId:"text-5",type:"reading",question:"Some people do not openly admit they have ambition because____.",options:{A:"they think of it as immoral",B:"their pursuits are not fame or wealth",C:"ambition is not closely related to material benefits",D:"they do not want to appear greedy and contemptible"},answer:"D",analysis:'【判型】Some people do not openly admit they have ambition because____. 是 because 引导 【拆句】Some people do not openly admit they have ambition because____. 主句+because 【定位】P2 What has happened is that people cannot confess fully to their dreams, as easily and openly as once they could, lest they be thought pushing, acquisitive and vulgar. 【选项】A) 过度推断 they think of it as immoral 把原文"怕被人看成贪婪庸俗"（社交评价/面子）拔高到 原文整段强调人们 do not seem less interested in success and its signs——对成功 的标志（避暑别墅、欧洲游、BMW）兴趣未减；their pursuits are not fame or ambition is not closely related to material benefits 谈的是 ambition 与物质回报的 wealth/Summer homes/BMW 紧密绑定，反向也站不住。 D) ✓ 正确 lest they be thought pushing, acquisitive and vulgar ↔ they do not want to appear greedy and contemptible they do not want to appear greedy and contemptible 既对上了原文 "lest…be thought" 的动机结构，又对上了 acquisitive/vulgar 的形容词语义场。',position:0},{id:30,articleId:"text-5",type:"reading",question:"From the last paragraph the conclusion can be drawn that ambition should be maintained____. Part B Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points) Governments throughout the world act on the assumption that the welfare of their people depends largely on the economic strength and wealth of the community. (31)Under modern conditions, this requires varying measures of centralized control and hence the help of specialized scientists such as economists and operational research experts. (32)Furthermore, it is obvious that the strength of a country's economy is directly bound up with the efficiency of its agriculture and industry, and that this in turn rests upon the efforts of scientists and technologists of all kinds. It also means that governments are increasingly compelled to interfere in these sectors in order to step up production and ensure that it is utilized to the best advantage. For example, they may encourage research in various ways, including the setting up of their own research centers; they may alter the structure of education, or interfere in order to reduce the wastage of natural resources or tap resources hitherto unexploited; or they may cooperate directly in the growing number of international projects related to science, economics and industry. In any case, all such interventions are heavily dependent on scientific advice and also scientific and technological manpower of all kinds. (33)Owing to the remarkable development in mass-communications, people everywhere are feeling new wants and are being exposed to new customs and ideas, while governments are often forced to introduce still further innovations for the reasons given above. At the same time, the normal rate of social change throughout the world is taking place at a vastly accelerated speed compared with the past. For example, (34)in the early industrialized countries of Europe the process of industrialization—with all the far-reaching changes in social patterns that followed—was spread over nearly a century, whereas nowadays a developing nation may undergo the same process in a decade or so. All this has the effect of building up unusual pressures and tensions within the community and consequently presents serious problems for the governments concerned. (35)Additional social stresses may also occur because of the population explosion or problems arising from mass migration movements —themselves made relatively easy nowadays by modern means of transport. As a result of all these factors, governments are becoming increasingly dependent on biologists and social scientists for planning the appropriate programs and putting them into effect.",options:{A:"secretly and vigorously",B:"openly and enthusiastically",C:"easily and momentarily",D:"verbally and spiritually"},answer:"B",analysis:`【判型】the conclusion can be drawn 显示本题不是直接细节，而是要从末段事实推出作者的隐含 【拆句】From the last paragraph the conclusion can be drawn that ambition should be maintained____. 是 "From+地点状语+主句+that 同位语从句" 结构；待补全的是 maintained 之后的方式状语（副词），暗示选项是 "副词+副词" 组合。 【定位】P3 This does not mean that ambition is at an end, that people no longer feel its stirrings and promptings, but only that, no longer openly honored, it is less openly professed. Consequences follow from this, of course, some of which are that ambition is driven underground, or made sly. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 secretly and vigorously 里的 secretly 恰好是作者所批评的 driven underground, or B) ✓ 正确 no longer openly honored, it is less openly professed ↔ openly and enthusiastically ambition is driven underground, or made sly ↔ openly and enthusiastically openly and enthusiastically 一头复现作者赞许的 openly，一头取反作者批评的 underground/sly，正反两面同时贴合，是末段立场的完整还原。 easily and momentarily 中的 momentarily（一瞬间地、短暂地）在末段完全无对应， 作者主张的是长期持续地维护 ambition，而非 "短暂地"；easily 也偏"轻松/随便"，与 verbally and spiritually 把作者主张的 "openly（公开地、明面上）" 偷换成 "verbally （口头上）"——但作者全文恰恰在批判 Succeed at all costs but avoid appearing 场；spiritually（精神上）原文亦无对应。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 9 If ambition is to be well regarded, the rewards of ambition—wealth, distinction, control over one's destiny—must be deemed worthy of the sacrifices made on ambition's behalf. In an odd way, however, it is the educated who have claimed to have given up on ambition as an ideal. There is a heavy note of hypocrisy in this, a case of closing the barn doo...`,position:0}]}],s={id:e,year:2e3,subject:t,title:a,sections:i};export{s as default,e as id,i as sections,t as subject,a as title,o as year};
